  "justification": "Evaluation of the Stage 3 architecture against requirements N1–N6 and the AWS Well-Architected Framework pillars:\n\n**Operational Excellence:** The original architecture supports monitoring via ALB metrics, RDS activity logs, and Auto Scaling group tracking. The proposed design extends this by increasing ASG max capacity from 4 to 10, enabling better instrumentation of scaling behavior under peak load and clearer operational patterns. Both designs integrate with CloudWatch for alarms and dashboards. The t3.small upgrade does not degrade operational tooling; Auto Scaling policies remain the primary operational lever in both cases.\n\n**Security:** Both architectures maintain identical security controls: compute instances in private subnets, internet-facing ALB as the sole ingress point, NAT Gateway for egress, and Security Groups enforcing least-privilege access. CloudFront, ALB, and RDS support encryption in transit and at rest. Network segmentation via subnets and security groups is unchanged. The proposed design introduces no new credentials, attack surface, or authentication requirements; the instance-type upgrade is purely a compute capacity change.\n\n**Reliability:** The original architecture with t3.micro instances (40 req/s per instance at 70% CPU) and ASG range 2–4 provides 80–160 req/s capacity. Requirement N1 mandates 750 req/s origin traffic at peak (1,500 req/s CloudFront demand with 50% cache hit). The original capacity is undersized by a factor of ~4.7×, causing request queuing, timeouts, and failures under peak load. The proposed architecture upgrades to t3.small instances (80 req/s per instance), scaling from 160 to 800 req/s capacity, directly meeting the 750 req/s peak requirement. Both architectures deploy to two Availability Zones (N2), satisfy Multi-AZ RDS failover (N2), and enable automated backups with 7-day retention for PITR (N3: RPO ≤5 min via continuous incremental backups, RTO ≤1 hour via managed failover).\n\n**Performance Efficiency:** t3.small instances provide double the throughput of t3.micro (80 vs. 40 req/s) at double per-instance cost. This right-sizing directly addresses peak-load sustainability (N1). With 50% CloudFront cache hit rate (N1), the origin tier avoids serving 750 req/s directly; without caching, sustaining 1,500 req/s would require ~19 t3.small instances. The proposed ASG max of 10 instances balances elasticity with cost constraints: sufficient spare capacity to handle demand spikes smoothly, and sufficient headroom to scale down to the minimum of 2 during steady state. Using t3.micro to meet peak demand would require 19–20 instances at $15.19 × (19/2) per month in compute alone, exceeding the N5 budget of $750 immediately.\n\n**Cost Optimization:** Original BOM cost calculation (price sheet values): EC2 t3.micro 1460 h × $0.0104 = $15.19; ALB 730 h × $0.0225 = $16.43; ALB LCU 1460 h × $0.008 = $11.68; NAT hour 730 h × $0.045 = $32.85; NAT GB 100 × $0.045 = $4.50; RDS hour Multi-AZ (730 h × $0.017 × 2.0) = $24.82; RDS storage Multi-AZ (20 GB × $0.115 × 2.0) = $4.60; S3 50 × $0.023 = $1.15; CloudFront GB 200 × $0.085 = $17.00; CloudFront requests 500 × $0.01 = $5.00; Total = $533.30. Proposed BOM cost: EC2 t3.small 1460 h × $0.0208 = $30.37; ALB $16.43; ALB LCU $11.68; NAT hour $32.85; NAT GB 50 × $0.045 = $2.25; RDS hour Multi-AZ (1460 h × $0.017 × 2.0) = $49.64; RDS storage Multi-AZ (40 GB × $0.115 × 2.0) = $9.20; S3 $1.15; CloudFront GB 2000 × $0.085 = $170.00; CloudFront requests 4000 × $0.01 = $40.00; Total = $363.57 + $370.58 = $734.15. The proposed cost remains within N5 budget of $750, with $15.85 contingency.",
